# Design to Code

Translate a Figma frame into the project's own UI components. Mapping, not
freehand interpretation. The designer works in Figma with a shared shadcn/ui
kit; you reuse the project's primitives and tokens so output looks hand-written.

## Handoff inputs (usually a GitHub Issue)

- Frame export PNG (primary — read it directly).
- Figma frame link (reference).
- Intent note naming kit components, variants, and interactions.
- Target feature location.

Free Figma: no Dev Mode MCP, no Variables API. Work from the image and the
shared kit vocabulary. A Figma REST token, if the project documents one, is an
optional precision pass for exact measurements — never required.

## Process

1. Read the project's tokens (`globals.css` / CSS variables) and primitive kit
   (`src/shared/components/ui/`) before writing anything.
2. Decompose the frame into kit primitives + feature composition.
3. Map every visual to a token — never hardcode a color, radius, or font a
   token covers.
4. Reuse primitives before creating. A missing primitive is scaffolded from
   shadcn/ui wired to existing tokens, then reported as added.
5. Compose feature components matching existing conventions (CVA, `cn`).
6. Verify: `tsc --noEmit`, lint, run.

## Constraints

- No invented tokens or parallel design language.
- When the design contradicts the kit, surface the gap — do not guess.
- Respect SPEC rules (R1.1 ≤300 lines, R1.4 strict TS, R3.5 no push without
  confirmation, R1.2 never read `.env`).

If native sub-agents are unavailable, run this role from
`.claude/agents/design-to-code-agent.md`.
